Name
    nginx_http_upstream_check_module - support upstream health check with
    Nginx
    

Synopsis
    http {

        upstream cluster {

            # simple round-robin
            server 192.168.0.1:80;
            server 192.168.0.2:80;

            check interval=5000 rise=1 fall=3 timeout=4000;

            #check interval=3000 rise=2 fall=5 timeout=1000 type=ssl_hello;

            #check interval=3000 rise=2 fall=5 timeout=1000 type=http;
            #check_http_send "HEAD / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n";
            #check_http_expect_alive http_2xx http_3xx;
        }

        server {
            listen 80;

            location / {
                proxy_pass http://cluster;
            }

            location /hmstatus {
                check_status;

                access_log   off;
                allow SOME.IP.ADD.RESS;
                deny all;
           }
        }

    }

    stream {

        upstream db {
            server 192.168.0.1:3306;
            server 192.168.0.2:3306;

            check interval=3000 rise=2 fall=5 timeout=1000 type=mysql;
        }

        server {
            listen 3306;
            proxy_pass db;
        }
    }

Description
    Add the support of health check with the upstream servers, in
    http { upstream {} } and, without the core patch, in
    stream { upstream {} } (see "Stream upstreams" below).

Directives
  check
    syntax: *check interval=milliseconds [fall=count] [rise=count]
    [timeout=milliseconds] [default_down=true|false]
    [type=tcp|http|ssl_hello|mysql|ajp|fastcgi]*

    default: *none, if parameters omitted, default parameters are
    interval=30000 fall=5 rise=2 timeout=1000 default_down=true type=tcp*

    context: *upstream* (http, stream)

    description: Add the health check for the upstream servers.

    The parameters' meanings are:

    *   *interval*: the check request's interval time.

    *   *fall*(fall_count): After fall_count check failures, the server is
        marked down.

    *   *rise*(rise_count): After rise_count check success, the server is
        marked up.

    *   *timeout*: the check request's timeout.

    *   *default_down*: set initial state of backend server, default is
        down.

    *   *port*: specify the check port in the backend servers. It can be
        different with the original servers port. Default the port is 0 and
        it means the same as the original backend server.

    *   *type*: the check protocol type:

        1.  *tcp* is a simple tcp socket connect and peek one byte.

        2.  *ssl_hello* sends a client ssl hello packet and receives the
            server ssl hello packet.

        3.  *http* sends a http request packet, receives and parses the http
            response to diagnose if the upstream server is alive.

        4.  *mysql* connects to the mysql server, receives the greeting
            response to diagnose if the upstream server is alive.

        5.  *ajp* sends a AJP Cping packet, receives and parses the AJP
            Cpong response to diagnose if the upstream server is alive.

        6.  *fastcgi* send a fastcgi request, receives and parses the
            fastcgi response to diagnose if the upstream server is alive.
            This type is only available in http upstreams.

    Each server is checked once per interval, however many worker
    processes there are. If the same server (address and port) is listed
    in several upstreams, or twice in one, and all its check settings are
    the same (check, check_keepalive_requests, check_http_send,
    check_http_expect_alive and check_fastcgi_param), it is checked only
    once and all its entries share the result. They are still listed
    separately on the status page. If any of the settings differ, e.g. a
    different Host header in check_http_send, each upstream checks the
    server on its own.

  check_http_send
    syntax: *check_http_send http_packet*

    default: *"GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n"*

    context: *upstream* (http, stream)

    description: If you set the check type is http, then the check function
    will sends this http packet to check the upstream server.

  check_http_expect_alive
    syntax: *check_http_expect_alive [ http_2xx | http_3xx | http_4xx |
    http_5xx ]*

    default: *http_2xx | http_3xx*

    context: *upstream* (http, stream)

    description: These status codes indicate the upstream server's http
    response is ok, the backend is alive.

  check_keepalive_requests
    syntax: *check_keepalive_requests num*

    default: *check_keepalive_requests 1*

    context: *upstream* (http, stream)

    description: The directive specifies the number of requests sent on a
    connection, the default vaule 1 indicates that nginx will certainly
    close the connection after a request.

  check_fastcgi_param
    Syntax: *check_fastcgi_params parameter value*

    default: see below

    context: *upstream* (http only)

    description: If you set the check type is fastcgi, then the check
    function will sends this fastcgi headers to check the upstream server.
    The default directive looks like:

          check_fastcgi_param "REQUEST_METHOD" "GET";
          check_fastcgi_param "REQUEST_URI" "/";
          check_fastcgi_param "SCRIPT_FILENAME" "index.php";

  check_shm_size
    syntax: *check_shm_size size*

    default: *1M*

    context: *http*, *stream*

    description: Default size is one megabytes. If you check thousands of
    servers, the shared memory for health check may be not enough, you can
    enlarge it with this directive. The http and the stream upstreams share
    one zone: if both blocks set it, the larger size is used.

  check_status
    syntax: *check_status [html|csv|json]*

    default: *none*

    context: *location*

    description: Display the health checking servers' status by HTTP. This
    directive should be set in the http block.

    You can specify the default display format. The formats can be `html`,
    `csv` or `json`. The default type is `html`. It also supports to specify
    the format by the request argument. Suppose your `check_status` location
    is '/hmstatus', the argument of `format` can change the display page's
    format. You can do like this:

        /hmstatus?format=html
        /hmstatus?format=csv
        /hmstatus?format=json

    The servers of the stream upstreams are listed on the same page. The
    last column ("Upstream type" in html, the last field in csv,
    "upstream_type" in json) is "http" or "stream", since an http and a
    stream upstream may have the same name. There is no check_status in
    the stream block: put it in an http server, even if all the checked
    upstreams are stream ones.

    At present, you can fetch the list of servers with the same status by
    the argument of `status`. For example:

        /hmstatus?format=html&status=down
        /hmstatus?format=csv&status=up

    Below it's the sample html page:

        <!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Strict//EN
        "http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-strict.dtd">
        <title>Nginx http upstream check status</title>
            <h1>Nginx http upstream check status</h1>
            <h2>Check upstream server number: 1, generation: 3</h2>
                    <th>Index</th>
                    <th>Upstream</th>
                    <th>Name</th>
                    <th>Status</th>
                    <th>Rise counts</th>
                    <th>Fall counts</th>
                    <th>Check type</th>
                    <th>Check port</th>
                    <th>Upstream type</th>
                    <td>0</td>
                    <td>backend</td>
                    <td>106.187.48.116:80</td>
                    <td>up</td>
                    <td>39</td>
                    <td>0</td>
                    <td>http</td>
                    <td>80</td>
                    <td>http</td>

    Below it's the sample of csv page:

        0,backend,106.187.48.116:80,up,46,0,http,80,http

    Below it's the sample of json page:

        {"servers": {
          "total": 1,
          "generation": 3,
          "server": [
           {"index": 0, "upstream": "backend", "name": "106.187.48.116:80", "status": "up", "rise": 58, "fall": 0, "type": "http", "port": 80, "upstream_type": "http"}
          ]
         }}

Installation
    Download the latest version of the release tarball of this module from
    github (<http://github.com/yaoweibin/nginx_upstream_check_module>)

    Grab the nginx source code from nginx.org (<http://nginx.org/>), and
    build it with this module. Since nginx 1.9.0 no patch to the nginx core
    is needed, and the module can be built either statically or as a
    dynamic module:

        $ wget 'http://nginx.org/download/nginx-1.30.6.tar.gz'
        $ tar -xzvf nginx-1.30.6.tar.gz
        $ cd nginx-1.30.6/

        $ ./configure --add-module=/path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module
        or
        $ ./configure --add-dynamic-module=/path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module

        $ make
        $ make install

    For the dynamic module, load it at the top of nginx.conf:

        load_module modules/ngx_http_upstream_check_module.so;

    When nginx is configured with --with-stream, the checks for the stream
    upstreams are built too, as ngx_stream_upstream_check_module. As a
    dynamic module it is a separate file, which uses the http module and
    must be loaded after it (and, with --with-stream=dynamic, after the
    stream module):

        load_module modules/ngx_stream_module.so;    # --with-stream=dynamic
        load_module modules/ngx_http_upstream_check_module.so;
        load_module modules/ngx_stream_upstream_check_module.so;

    With --with-stream=dynamic, the stream checks are only built with
    --add-dynamic-module. They are not built with the patched core.

Choosing a method
    The module can work on stock nginx (no patch, nginx 1.9.0+) or with a
    patched nginx core (one of the check_*.patch files). The "config"
    script picks the method by looking at the nginx sources: if the core
    is patched it builds the patched method, otherwise the stock one.

                                     stock nginx         patched core
                                     (no patch)
    nginx versions                   1.9.0+              any version with
                                                         a matching patch
    --add-dynamic-module             yes (1.9.11+)       no, --add-module only
    re-patch for new nginx releases  no                  yes
    http upstreams                   yes                 yes
    stream upstreams                 yes                 no
    servers added at run time        checked             not checked
      ("resolve", nginx-upsync-module,
      nginx-stream-upsync-module)
    built-in balancers (round robin, yes                 yes (random: no)
      hash, ip_hash, least_conn,
      random)
    third party balancers patched    no                  yes
      for this module (upstream fair,
      sticky, jvm_route)
    a failed server leaves the       within one sync     on the next
      balancers                      period (at most     request
                                     500ms)

    Use stock nginx unless you need nginx older than 1.9.0, or one of the
    third party balancers patched for this module, or cannot accept a
    failed server taking requests for up to one sync period. The run time
    servers, the stream upstreams and the dynamic module are only
    available on stock nginx.

    With the patched core, nginx-upsync-module's own check hooks call
    ngx_http_upstream_check_add_dynamic_peer(), which this module does not
    provide, so nginx-upsync-module cannot be built together with this
    module on a patched core.

Note
    How it works without the patch: when the configuration is loaded, the
    module registers the servers of every upstream with "check" from the
    upstream's round robin peers, matching them by address. Every worker
    then copies the check status from the shared memory into the peers'
    "down" flag at least every 500 milliseconds (or every half of the
    smallest check interval). All the built-in load balancing methods
    (round robin, hash, hash consistent, ip_hash, least_conn and random)
    skip peers with this flag set, with or without "zone". Servers marked
    "down" in the configuration are not checked and are never marked up.

    Servers which show up in such an upstream at run time are checked too
    (on stock nginx only, the patched core does not check them),
    for example servers resolved with the "resolve" parameter (nginx
    1.27.3+) or added by nginx-upsync-module. The sync notices a peer it
    has no check for, and adds a check for its address in a slot of the
    shared memory, shared by all the workers. The new check starts in the
    "default_down" state. Once no worker has seen the address in the
    upstream for 10 check intervals (at least 10 seconds), the slot is
    released. The state of these checks is kept over a reload, and they
    are listed on the status page like the configured servers. Half of
    "check_shm_size" (1M by default, about 1000 slots) is reserved for
    them; raise "check_shm_size" if nginx logs "no free slot to check
    peer".

    Limitations without the patch:

    *   Third party load balancing methods which do not keep the round
        robin peers in the upstream (for example upstream fair) are not
        supported; nginx logs a warning and the "check" of such an
        upstream is ignored. Use the patched core for them.

    *   A server goes down in the balancers up to one sync period after
        the check marks it down. A server added at run time takes traffic
        until the next sync, when it gets its "default_down" state, and a
        peer re-created by the resolver starts as up until the next sync.

    *   nginx-upsync-module (and nginx-stream-upsync-module): servers in
        the file named by "upsync_dump_path" are configured servers once
        nginx reloads, and stay checked (and listed on the status page)
        until the next reload, even if upsync removes them. Its own check
        hooks (NGX_HTTP_UPSTREAM_CHECK) are not used, nor needed, without
        the patch.

    Stream upstreams: the "check" directives work the same way in
    stream { upstream {} }, with the check types tcp, http, ssl_hello,
    mysql and ajp. The stream round robin peers have the same "down" flag,
    which the stream balancers (round robin, hash, hash consistent,
    least_conn and random) honour, so the stream servers are checked and
    marked down as above, with or without "zone", including servers added
    at run time by "resolve" (nginx 1.27.3+) and by
    nginx-stream-upsync-module. The check engine, its shared memory, the
    slots for the run time servers and the status page are shared with the
    http upstreams. The stream checks need stock nginx: with a patched
    core they are not built.

    The tests for the unpatched mode are in test/patchless/.

    Patching the nginx core, as in the older versions of this module, is
    still supported. The "config" script detects a patched core and then
    builds the module in the patched mode (NGX_HTTP_UPSTREAM_CHECK is
    defined and the balancers call into the module directly). The patched
    mode needs --add-module, it cannot be built as a dynamic module. It is
    required for nginx versions before 1.9.0 and for the patched third
    party modules below. To use it, apply the patch matching your nginx
    version before running configure:

        $ patch -p1 < /path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module/check_1.20.1+.patch

    If you use nginx-1.2.1 or nginx-1.3.0, the nginx upstream round robin
    module changed greatly. You should use the patch named
    'check_1.2.1.patch'.

    If you use nginx-1.2.2+ or nginx-1.3.1+, It added the upstream
    least_conn module. You should use the patch named 'check_1.2.2+.patch'.

    If you use nginx-1.2.6+ or nginx-1.3.9+, It adjusted the round robin
    module. You should use the patch named 'check_1.2.6+.patch'.

    If you use nginx-1.5.12+, You should use the patch named
    'check_1.5.12+.patch'.

    If you use nginx-1.7.2+, You should use the patch named
    'check_1.7.2+.patch'.

    If you use nginx-1.20.1+, You should use the patch named
    'check_1.20.1+.patch'.

    If you use nginx-1.30.4+ (the current stable branch, which merged the
    upstream sticky 'sid' code and added per-peer locking into the round
    robin / hash / ip_hash modules), You should use the patch named
    'check_1.30.4+.patch'.

    The patch just adds the support for the official Round-Robin, Ip_hash
    and least_conn upstream module. But it's easy to expand my module to
    other upstream modules. See the patch for detail.

    If you want to add the support for upstream fair module, you can do it
    like this:

        $ git clone git://github.com/gnosek/nginx-upstream-fair.git
        $ cd nginx-upstream-fair
        $ patch -p2 < /path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module/upstream_fair.patch
        $ cd /path/to/nginx-1.0.14
        $ ./configure --add-module=/path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module --add-module=/path/to/nginx-upstream-fair-module
        $ make
        $ make install

    If you want to add the support for nginx sticky module, you can do it
    like this:

        $ svn checkout http://nginx-sticky-module.googlecode.com/svn/trunk/ nginx-sticky-module
        $ cd nginx-sticky-module
        $ patch -p0 < /path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module/nginx-sticky-module.patch
        $ cd /path/to/nginx-1.0.14
        $ ./configure --add-module=/path/to/nginx_http_upstream_check_module --add-module=/path/to/nginx-sticky-module
        $ make
        $ make install

    Note that, the nginx-sticky-module also needs the original check.patch.

Compatibility
    *   The module version 0.1.5 should be compatibility with 0.7.67+

    *   The module version 0.1.8 should be compatibility with Nginx-1.0.14+

